Chlamydia trachomatis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Often asymptomatic, Mucopurulent urethral/cervical discharge, Dysuria, Postcoital spotting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-002
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe lower abdominal discomfort and deep pelvic pain requiring urgent assessment to rule out surgical emergencies such as ectopic pregnancy or ovarian torsion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute and progressive lower pelvic pain accompanied by abnormal discharge and bleeding. Urgent physical assessment is required to exclude life-threatening acute pelvic pathology and prevent potential ascending infection (PID) complications prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower stomach has a deep, sharp pain and it burns when I pass urine."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 72 hours ago with dysuria characterized by a burning sensation during micturition, evolving over the last 24 hours into deep pelvic pain and lower abdominal discomfort. The patient noted purulent discharge and an episode of postcoital spotting yesterday. Denies prior similar episodes. The progressive nature of the deep pelvic pain raises high suspicion for evolving pelvic inflammatory disease (PID).
Functional Status: Independent, but currently resting in bed due to pain exacerbated by movement.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; expresses significant distress regarding potential fertility implications.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, appearing anxious, visibly wincing when adjusting position in bed.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft with marked suprapubic and bilateral lower quadrant tenderness. No rebound or rigidity. Bowel sounds are normal. Visual inspection confirms mucopurulent discharge. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Often asymptomatic (though currently symptomatic), Mucopurulent urethral/cervical discharge, Dysuria, and Postcoital spotting.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean environment. No obvious trip hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: On oral contraceptives. No other regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chlamydia trachomatis (presumptive with evolving PID) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ectopic pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Negative home urine hCG test performed during the visit.

Ovarian torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral nature of the pain and absence of sudden, severe, unilateral agonizing pain with intractable vomiting.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of purulent discharge and postcoital spotting, which are atypical for uncomplicated cystitis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with acute lower pelvic pain, dysuria, and purulent discharge. The presence of deep pelvic pain necessitates urgent clinical evaluation to rule out critical acute abdomen differentials including ectopic pregnancy and appendicitis. Given the high risk of rapid progression to severe pelvic inflammatory disease and subsequent tubal scarring, delaying clinical assessment and empirical antibiotic initiation until the next in-hours period was clinically unsafe. This justifies an urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe empirical oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily for 7 days. Provide a referral for a first-catch urine sample and endocervical/vaginal swab for NAAT to confirm the diagnosis tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the antibiotic exactly as prescribed with food, the patient will avoid gastric upset, which will serve as positive reinforcement for medication adherence and ensure complete eradication of the pathogen.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to confirm symptom improvement and review swab results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops severe right upper quadrant pain (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome), high fever over 39.0 degrees, intractable vomiting, or an inability to tolerate oral fluids and medications.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending NAAT swab results; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Pain and discharge should markedly improve within 48 to 72 hours of initiating antibiotic therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if your pain becomes suddenly unbearable, if you feel faint or dizzy, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The antibiotics will halt the bacterial replication. It is critical to treat this aggressively to prevent long-term complications such as chronic pelvic pain or infertility.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire course of antibiotics. Abstain from sexual contact until 7 days after completing the treatment and ensure all recent sexual partners are tested and treated.

